Coming up,

458
00:34:39,241 --> 00:34:47,840
these caregiving canines make sure our country's finest get the hero's treatment they deserve.

459
00:34:47,841 --> 00:34:55,280
A German shepherd named Tracker found the last human survivor of the 9-11 terrorist attack, under 30 feet of the Twin Towers rubble.

460
00:34:56,220 --> 00:35:01,640
Tracker died in 2009, but scientists successfully produced five cloned puppies.

461
00:35:02,980 --> 00:35:05,360
Dogs will return on Modern Marvels.

462
00:35:07,240 --> 00:35:10,620
We now return to Dogs on Modern Marvels.

463
00:35:10,621 --> 00:35:16,060
President Harry Truman once famously commented,

464
00:35:16,660 --> 00:35:19,280
if you want a friend in Washington, get a dog.

465
00:35:19,500 --> 00:35:20,660
I'm going to call the dog Buddy.

466
00:35:21,100 --> 00:35:23,800
Nearly every chief executive has felt the same way.

467
00:35:24,680 --> 00:35:29,100
More than 50 canines have made the White House their home over the last two centuries.

468
00:35:29,620 --> 00:35:32,300
Dogs have been a part of the president's lives since the beginning.

469
00:35:32,301 --> 00:35:36,000
George Washington himself owned 10 American foxhounds.

470
00:35:36,260 --> 00:35:37,560
He actually invented the breed.

471
00:35:38,440 --> 00:35:43,900
Calvin Coolidge had more than ten dogs, including a collie named Boston Beans.

472
00:35:45,400 --> 00:35:48,840
LBJ's Mutt Yuki liked to sing with him in the Oval Office.

473
00:35:49,700 --> 00:35:57,280
And George Bush's Springer Spaniel Millie wrote a book, ghosted by the First Lady, that outsold her master's biography.

474
00:36:00,920 --> 00:36:04,680
FDR's Scottish terrier, Fallah, had his own press secretary.

475
00:36:04,681 --> 00:36:07,240
and during the election of 1944,

476
00:36:08,140 --> 00:36:10,720
even helped win his master a fourth term in office,

477
00:36:11,420 --> 00:36:13,480
despite a controversial turn of events.

478
00:36:13,760 --> 00:36:18,380
Republican critics accused FDR of spending millions of dollars

479
00:36:18,381 --> 00:36:20,900
going back to get Fowler from the Aleutian Islands.

480
00:36:21,320 --> 00:36:24,460
They claimed that Fowler had been left there,

481
00:36:24,900 --> 00:36:28,220
FDR realized the error, sent the Navy back.

482
00:36:28,221 --> 00:36:31,620
The accusation was untrue, and with the help of Fowler,

483
00:36:31,621 --> 00:36:34,820
President Roosevelt turned the tables on his opponents.

484
00:36:35,240 --> 00:36:37,040
He was given a speech to the Teamsters

485
00:36:37,041 --> 00:36:40,520
and he gave this overwhelmingly just rollicking speech

486
00:36:40,521 --> 00:36:44,480
about how you can talk about him, talk about his family,

487
00:36:44,700 --> 00:36:47,760
but leave little Fowler out of this.

488
00:36:47,860 --> 00:36:50,820
I think I have a right to resent,

489
00:36:51,760 --> 00:36:55,520
to object to libelous statements about my dog.

490
00:36:57,920 --> 00:37:00,320
Some historians believe the Fowler speech

491
00:37:00,321 --> 00:37:03,240
helped swing the 1944 election in FDR's favor.

492
00:37:03,840 --> 00:37:07,380
But that wasn't the only time a dog helped a politician win over voters.

493
00:37:08,040 --> 00:37:13,060
In 1952, Senator Richard Nixon faced accusations of illegal funding

494
00:37:13,061 --> 00:37:16,680
and went on the air to save his candidacy for vice president.

495
00:37:17,100 --> 00:37:21,280
I come before you tonight as a candidate for the vice presidency

496
00:37:21,281 --> 00:37:26,940
and as a man whose honesty and integrity has been questioned.

497
00:37:26,941 --> 00:37:32,420
The one thing that he did admit to receiving as a gift was his dog, Checkers.

498
00:37:33,260 --> 00:37:34,300
And he said...

499
00:37:34,301 --> 00:37:37,340
You know, the kids, like all kids, love the dog.

500
00:37:38,240 --> 00:37:43,780
And I just want to say this right now, that regardless of what they say about it, we're going to keep it.

501
00:37:44,100 --> 00:37:45,740
It endeared him to the American public,

502
00:37:45,800 --> 00:37:51,800
and letters poured in about how Eisenhower should keep Nixon on the 52 presidential ticket.

503
00:37:51,801 --> 00:37:57,720
Dogs have served America as more than just political allies and faithful companions to the president.

504
00:38:00,220 --> 00:38:03,820
When America has gone to war, so have its dogs.

505
00:38:07,820 --> 00:38:14,060
During World War II, dogs carried messages across battle lines, alerted soldiers of hidden explosives,

506
00:38:14,580 --> 00:38:17,620
and sniffed out ambushes from up to a thousand yards away,

507
00:38:17,980 --> 00:38:20,920
a skill that saved countless American lives in the Pacific theater.

508
00:38:20,921 --> 00:38:24,748
In the Pacific, the fighting was very close. It was hand-to-hand.

509
00:38:24,749 --> 00:38:27,280
There were jungles. It was often very dark.

510
00:38:28,120 --> 00:38:32,200
And dogs would be used to try to find and ascertain the location of the enemy.

511
00:38:32,420 --> 00:38:37,260
Units that used dogs actually suffered lower casualty rates because of their use.

512
00:38:37,780 --> 00:38:39,959
Dogs proved so useful in World War II.

513
00:38:39,960 --> 00:38:43,800
They've served alongside American soldiers in every conflict since.

514
00:38:44,800 --> 00:38:51,860
They've served in Korea, they've served in Vietnam, they've served in Afghanistan, and they continue to serve today.

515
00:38:53,040 --> 00:38:58,320
Like their predecessors, modern war dogs serve as guards or search and rescue scouts.

516
00:38:59,000 --> 00:39:04,920
They've also proven to be the best means of finding improvised explosive devices, or IEDs.

517
00:39:04,921 --> 00:39:10,940
Even with our modern technology, our laser-guided munitions, our modern implements of war,

518
00:39:11,260 --> 00:39:15,760
we still can't replace the special skills of a dog.

519
00:39:16,400 --> 00:39:18,060
Come here. Okay, sit.

520
00:39:18,800 --> 00:39:22,480
But American military dogs are also serving their country in a new way,

521
00:39:22,840 --> 00:39:28,060
helping afflicted veterans on the home front heal from psychological and physical trauma.

522
00:39:28,280 --> 00:39:30,300
That's a good healer. Good boy.

523
00:39:30,500 --> 00:39:32,840
It's called Paws for Purple Hearts.

524
00:39:32,841 --> 00:39:34,880
Nice. Good boy.

525
00:39:34,920 --> 00:39:46,033
The Postoperable Hearts program is based on a symbiotic relationship where one group of veterans with psychological injuries meet the needs of another group of

526
00:39:46,034 --> 00:39:48,060
veterans with physical needs.

527
00:39:48,620 --> 00:39:49,560
Good, wait.

528
00:39:50,180 --> 00:39:56,440
Volunteer trainers like Iraqi war veteran Jason Baker prepare dogs to assist physically disabled veterans.

529
00:39:56,580 --> 00:39:59,620
Josh, pull, pull. That's it. That's it. Come on, pull.

530
00:39:59,621 --> 00:40:03,780
In return, Jason, who suffers from post-traumatic stress disorder,

531
00:40:04,400 --> 00:40:05,780
receives some treatment as well.

532
00:40:06,340 --> 00:40:08,800
Hey, are you ready to train? Huh? You ready? Go.

533
00:40:09,240 --> 00:40:14,440
I came home and started having a hard time being in large crowds of people,

534
00:40:14,680 --> 00:40:17,200
just overall depression.

535
00:40:17,600 --> 00:40:20,860
Once I started working with these dogs and I found out that these dogs

536
00:40:20,861 --> 00:40:24,700
were actually being trained to help fellow veterans,

537
00:40:25,160 --> 00:40:26,440
it gave me a sense of purpose.

538
00:40:26,441 --> 00:40:31,640
It gave me something positive in my life to work towards and a really good feeling.

539
00:40:33,200 --> 00:40:34,840
Part of that good feeling...

540
00:40:34,841 --> 00:40:36,140
That's it. Almost.

541
00:40:37,120 --> 00:40:38,160
Flip with your nose. Yes!

542
00:40:38,400 --> 00:40:40,200
Good boy. Very nice.

543
00:40:40,220 --> 00:40:45,080
...comes from the fact that Jason is training the dogs to help his fellow soldiers with everyday life.

544
00:40:45,480 --> 00:40:51,720
Okay, so one task that people take for granted that a veteran in a wheelchair might not be able to do

545
00:40:51,721 --> 00:40:53,960
is retrieve a set of car keys.

546
00:40:53,961 --> 00:40:57,900
Somebody in a wheelchair drops their keys.

547
00:40:57,901 --> 00:41:00,120
They can't bend over to pick them up,

548
00:41:00,121 --> 00:41:02,460
so they can ask the dog to pick them up.

549
00:41:02,461 --> 00:41:03,340
So I'll show you.

550
00:41:03,341 --> 00:41:06,140
Girl, look, go get it.

551
00:41:06,141 --> 00:41:08,200
That's it, that's it, bring it here.

552
00:41:08,201 --> 00:41:10,500
That's it, good girl, come here, lap.

553
00:41:10,501 --> 00:41:13,640
That's it, oh good girl, thank you very much.

554
00:41:13,641 --> 00:41:15,040
Look, tug.

555
00:41:15,041 --> 00:41:16,780
Jason also trains the dogs

556
00:41:16,781 --> 00:41:18,580
to complete more complex tasks,

557
00:41:18,581 --> 00:41:20,420
like opening the refrigerator.

558
00:41:20,421 --> 00:41:24,960
and retrieving food or drinks.

559
00:41:28,320 --> 00:41:30,140
Once a dog has completed his training,

560
00:41:30,760 --> 00:41:32,600
he's paired with a physically disabled vet.

561
00:41:35,360 --> 00:41:39,300
U.S. Army veteran Bill Smith lost the use of his legs

562
00:41:39,301 --> 00:41:42,980
when his Jeep careened into a ravine during a training exercise.

563
00:41:43,440 --> 00:41:45,820
But Bill gained a new perspective on life

564
00:41:45,821 --> 00:41:47,580
when he was matched with Benuto.

565
00:41:47,581 --> 00:41:50,380
All the physical things he does for me are wonderful.

566
00:41:50,580 --> 00:41:53,360
Good boy. Good job, Bobby.

567
00:41:53,540 --> 00:41:54,900
He can take my socks off.

568
00:41:55,000 --> 00:41:55,840
Get him. Tug.

569
00:41:56,660 --> 00:41:58,260
I drop my driver's license on the ground.

570
00:41:58,460 --> 00:42:00,160
He can pick up a driver's license.

571
00:42:00,580 --> 00:42:03,540
But what he's done for me emotionally, it can't be bought.

572
00:42:03,740 --> 00:42:05,020
It can't be measured.

573
00:42:05,200 --> 00:42:07,980
Good boy. Almost. Good boy.

574
00:42:08,240 --> 00:42:11,800
What Bill sees in Venuto is what we see in all our dogs.

575
00:42:12,480 --> 00:42:17,260
Strength, intelligence, and loyalty almost beyond our comprehension.

576
00:42:17,580 --> 00:42:25,260
And whether they're saving lives or tracking down criminals, wrangling our livestock, or rescuing us from danger,

577
00:42:25,500 --> 00:42:30,400
the bond between a dog and its master is one of unwavering love.

578
00:42:30,460 --> 00:42:36,360
I have no idea why dogs and men have such a common bond and relate so well to one another.

579
00:42:36,760 --> 00:42:39,280
I don't know where it came from. All I know is it's there.

580
00:42:39,281 --> 00:42:48,800
Dogs have made us better by being so giving and being willing to accept us for who we are.

581
00:42:48,920 --> 00:42:52,080
And in that process, they're helping us accept ourselves.

582
00:42:52,740 --> 00:42:54,420
You make me a better person, buddy.

583
00:42:54,980 --> 00:42:57,040
You make me a better guy. You really do.

584
00:42:57,200 --> 00:42:57,900
Right.

585
00:42:57,980 --> 00:42:58,860
Good girl.

